    {
      "id": "q-head",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 924,
      "y": 2064,
      "width": 900,
      "height": 20,
      "strokeColor": "#a15c1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "roughness": 0,
      "groupIds": [
        "Q",
        "lane3"
      ],
      "seed": 3302,
      "text": "QUESTIONS - deep links, and whether the desktop can have them at all",
      "fontSize": 15,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "autoResize": false,
      "index": "a231",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 0,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790251001522,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "QUESTIONS - deep links, and whether the desktop can have them at all",
      "lineHeight": 1.3333333333333333
    },
    {
      "id": "q-body",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 924,
      "y": 2098,
      "width": 1490,
      "height": 130,
      "strokeColor": "#4b5563",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "roughness": 0,
      "groupIds": [
        "Q",
        "lane3"
      ],
      "seed": 3303,
      "text": "1.  Desktop has no scheme handler today. Nothing in the desktop module registers one and nothing handles it: appOpenUrl is wired in commonMain but has no desktop plumbing, and\n     desktop/build.gradle.kts declares only Deb, Dmg, Msi and Exe with no protocol entry. Adding one is per-OS work - CFBundleURLTypes plus setOpenURIHandler on macOS, registry keys\n     the MSI would have to write on Windows, a .desktop file with x-scheme-handler/simplexchat on Linux - and it would still not cover an unpackaged run. Hence D1: desktop does not wait for it.\n\n2.  Will a browser follow it without a tap? Safari and Chrome both refuse some scheme navigations that no user gesture asked for, and a refused one can show an error page rather\n     than nothing. That is what Return to SimpleX is for, and why Show code sits under it - but it decides whether the automatic attempt is worth making at all.",
      "fontSize": 10,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "autoResize": false,
      "index": "a232",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 0,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790251001522,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1.  Desktop has no scheme handler today. Nothing in the desktop module registers one and nothing handles it: appOpenUrl is wired in commonMain but has no desktop plumbing, and\n     desktop/build.gradle.kts declares only Deb, Dmg, Msi and Exe with no protocol entry. Adding one is per-OS work - CFBundleURLTypes plus setOpenURIHandler on macOS, registry keys\n     the MSI would have to write on Windows, a .desktop file with x-scheme-handler/simplexchat on Linux - and it would still not cover an unpackaged run. Hence D1: desktop does not wait for it.\n\n2.  Will a browser follow it without a tap? Safari and Chrome both refuse some scheme navigations that no user gesture asked for, and a refused one can show an error page rather\n     than nothing. That is what Return to SimpleX is for, and why Show code sits under it - but it decides whether the automatic attempt is worth making at all.",
      "lineHeight": 1.4444444444444444
    },
    {
      "id": "n-box",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 900,
      "y": 2340,
      "width": 1540,
      "height": 300,
      "strokeColor": "#c9ced6",
